window.docContents[35].resolve({"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Functors___-Applicative-Functors___-and-Monads--Summary--Universes":{"contents":"To allow Lean to be used as both a programming language and a theorem prover, some restrictions on the language are necessary.\nThis includes restrictions on recursive functions that ensure that they all either terminate or are marked as partial and written to return types that are not uninhabited.\nAdditionally, it must be impossible to represent certain kinds of logical paradoxes as types.\n\nOne of the restrictions that rules out certain paradoxes is that every type is assigned to a universe.\nUniverses are types such as , , , , and so forth.\nThese types describe other types—just as  and  are described by ,  is itself described by , and  is described by .\nThe type of functions that take a type as an argument must be a larger universe than the argument's universe.\n\nBecause each declared datatype has a universe, writing code that uses types like data would quickly become annoying, requiring each polymorphic type to be copy-pasted to take arguments from .\nA feature called universe polymorphism allows Lean programs and datatypes to take universe levels as arguments, just as ordinary polymorphism allows programs to take types as arguments.\nGenerally speaking, Lean libraries should use universe polymorphism when implementing libraries of polymorphic operations.\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009Summary","header":"5.7.4. Universes","id":"/#Functional-Programming-in-Lean--Functors___-Applicative-Functors___-and-Monads--Summary--Universes"},"/#universe-polymorphism":{"contents":"Defining a datatype in a specific universe can lead to code duplication.\nPlacing  in  means that it can't be used for an actual list of types.\nPlacing it in  means that it can't be used for a list of lists of types.\nRather than copy-pasting the datatype to create versions in , , , and so on, a feature called universe polymorphism can be used to write a single definition that can be instantiated in any of these universes.\n\nOrdinary polymorphic types use variables to stand for types in a definition.\nThis allows Lean to fill in the variables differently, which enables these definitions to be used with a variety of types.\nSimilarly, universe polymorphism allows variables to stand for universes in a definition, enabling Lean to fill them in differently so that they can be used with a variety of universes.\nJust as type arguments are conventionally named with Greek letters, universe arguments are conventionally named u, v, and w.\n\nThis definition of  doesn't specify a particular universe level, but instead uses a variable  to stand for any level.\nIf the resulting datatype is used with , then  is 0, and if it's used with , then  is 3:\n\n\n\nWith this definition, the same definition of  can be used to contain both actual natural numbers and the natural number type itself:\n\n\n\nIt can even contain itself:\n\n\n\nIt would seem that this would make it possible to write a logical paradox.\nAfter all, the whole point of the universe system is to rule out self-referential types.\nBehind the scenes, however, each occurrence of  is provided with a universe level argument.\nIn essence, the universe-polymorphic definition of  created a copy of the datatype at each level, and the level argument selects which copy is to be used.\nThese level arguments are written with a dot and curly braces, so , , and .\n\nWriting the levels explicitly, the prior example becomes:\n\n\n\nWhen a universe-polymorphic definition takes multiple types as arguments, it's a good idea to give each argument its own level variable for maximum flexibility.\nFor example, a version of  with a single level argument can be written as follows:\n\n\n\nThis definition can be used at multiple levels:\n\n\n\nHowever, it requires that both arguments be in the same universe:\n\n\n\n\n\nThis datatype can be made more flexible by using different variables for the two type arguments' universe levels, and then declaring that the resulting datatype is in the largest of the two:\n\n\n\nThis allows  to be used with arguments from different universes:\n\n\n\nIn positions where Lean expects a universe level, any of the following are allowed:\n\n* A concrete level, like 0 or 1* A variable that stands for a level, such as  or * The maximum of two levels, written as  applied to the levels* A level increase, written with \n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Functional Programming in Lean\u0009Functors, Applicative Functors, and Monads\u0009Universes","header":"5.5.2. Universe Polymorphism","id":"/#universe-polymorphism"}});